  "language": "en",
  "language_probability": 1,
  "text": "Your touchscreen does not need to feel pressure from your finger. It senses a tiny change in an electric field. Under the glass sits a grid of transparent electrodes. A small electrical signal moves through them. Your finger conducts a little charge and disturbs the field nearby. The controller measures which rows and columns change. Their crossing point becomes your touch. That is why a normal glove blocks the signal. A conductive glove works because it lets the field reach your skin, so the glass is not just watching your finger, it is listening for a tiny electrical disturbance.",
  "mean_word_probability": 0.983009180035254,
